1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
For some, this piece of California desert is hallowed ground.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
A land of firsts.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
America's first jet plane, first rocket plane,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
first flying wing, and first space shuttle landing.

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
It's where Chuck Yeager broke the sound barrier

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
and where the X-43 scramjet took jet power

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
to nearly 10 times the speed of sound.

8
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Edwards Air Force Base on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,740
Even from space, the huge dry lake at Edwards Air Force Base

10
00:00:41,741 --> 00:00:44,840
can be a comforting sight. Located in California's

11
00:00:44,841 --> 00:00:48,460
Antelope Valley, the 44-square-mile dry lake bed

12
00:00:48,461 --> 00:00:54,160
is a huge natural landing field. It's obvious when you're over

13
00:00:54,161 --> 00:00:59,260
this area, the lake bed's seven miles wide and 15 miles long,

14
00:00:59,261 --> 00:01:02,460
and it's bright, light, tan.

15
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:06,460
It stands out, so there's no missing it from orbit.

16
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:09,300
Test pilot and former astronaut Gordon Fullerton

17
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,000
piloted the space shuttle out of its orbit around the Earth

18
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:14,100
to a safe landing in Edwards.

19
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:18,020
Our reentry started over the Indian Ocean in the dark,

20
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:20,920
and so we were down much lower altitude

21
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:23,960
when the sun came up as we were crossing the South Pacific.

22
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:26,920
So the entry was mostly in daylight.

23
00:01:26,921 --> 00:01:29,760
Columbia, you got perfect energy, perfect ground track.

24
00:01:29,761 --> 00:01:31,760
You're up.

25
00:01:31,761 --> 00:01:34,260
What a way to come to California.

26
00:01:34,261 --> 00:01:37,260
And then that dry lake bed here at Edwards stood out.

27
00:01:37,261 --> 00:01:40,260
It's a terrific landmark for any aviator,

28
00:01:40,261 --> 00:01:43,160
and we're right on course.

29
00:01:43,161 --> 00:01:46,480
Over the past six decades, more major milestones

30
00:01:46,481 --> 00:01:50,380
in flight have occurred at this base than anywhere else on Earth.

31
00:01:50,381 --> 00:01:54,380
More than 150 airplanes have made their first flights here.

32
00:01:54,381 --> 00:01:58,860
Mach 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 were all exceeded here

33
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:00,920
for the first time.

34
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:03,460
From here, men first climbed in airplanes

35
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,900
above 100,000 feet, 200,000 feet, 300,000 feet.

36
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,560
It was here the space shuttle landed

37
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:14,640
after its first orbital flight in April of 1981.

38
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:17,100
One of the biggest new projects at Edwards

39
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:20,740
is the futuristic airborne laser weapon, which will be carried

40
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,440
aboard a Boeing 747.

41
00:02:23,441 --> 00:02:26,780
These airborne laser aircraft will patrol in pairs, scanning

42
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,440
the horizon for missiles.

43
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:30,940
If they detect one, a high-energy laser

44
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:36,180
will be fired at the missile in a three to five second burst.

45
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:38,780
This building houses the test program, which

46
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:42,400
is scheduled to continue until 2013.

47
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:45,500
It's at the south end of Rogers Dry Lake.

48
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,400
The airplane knows protruding from the building

49
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:49,100
is not just for decoration.

50
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:53,040
On the other side of the wall is a 747 fuselage used for ground

51
00:02:53,041 --> 00:02:57,680
testing the weapon. In front of the airborne laser

52
00:02:57,681 --> 00:03:01,680
building, in the parking lot, is a seldom noticed concrete pit

53
00:03:01,681 --> 00:03:05,120
that played a crucial role in breaking the sound barrier.

54
00:03:05,121 --> 00:03:08,720
Chuck Yeager's X-1 rocket plane was lowered into this pit to

55
00:03:08,721 --> 00:03:12,160
allow connection to the B-29 bomber that carried it aloft to

56
00:03:12,161 --> 00:03:16,000
break the sound barrier. The problem with the X-1 was

57
00:03:16,001 --> 00:03:21,200
that it was taller than the ground clearance of a B-29.

58
00:03:21,201 --> 00:03:31,540
So the simplest way was to erect a pit, back it down in there, taxi the mother plane over on top of it, and then just jack it up.

59
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:34,840
And having served its purpose, it's still here today.

60
00:03:34,841 --> 00:03:38,700
It fills with water every winter just about.

61
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:42,040
It's a playground for frogs, but it's still historic.

62
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:49,100
It's hard to avoid bumping into history all over the 300,000 acres that make up Edwards Air Force Base.

63
00:03:49,101 --> 00:03:53,100
The Air Force Flight Test Center, or AFFTC, has been the

64
00:03:53,101 --> 00:03:57,100
aeronautical equivalent of Mecca for more than 60 years.

65
00:03:57,101 --> 00:04:00,100
Nearly every aircraft to enter the Air Force inventory has been

66
00:04:00,101 --> 00:04:04,100
tested here. How and why did Edwards become

67
00:04:04,101 --> 00:04:07,100
the premier flight test center in America?

68
00:04:07,101 --> 00:04:10,100
The simple answer is a real estate cliche, location,

69
00:04:10,101 --> 00:04:14,100
location, location. Edwards wouldn't be Edwards

70
00:04:14,101 --> 00:04:18,100
without Rogers Dry Lake, which the base encompasses.

71
00:04:18,101 --> 00:04:21,700
The largest lake bed of its kind in the world, it's smoothed

72
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:24,740
perfectly flat by the winds that blow across it during the annual

73
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:27,840
winter rains. This lake bed, when it's dry,

74
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:31,740
can support a weight of 20,000 pounds to the square foot.

75
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:35,960
What this means is that any aircraft or spacecraft, now or

76
00:04:36,060 --> 00:04:40,460
visualized in the future, can land here perfectly safely.

77
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:43,900
Various runways are marked on the lake bed, one of them

78
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,940
more than seven miles long. A 15,000-foot

79
00:04:47,941 --> 00:04:52,740
concrete runway is located next to Rogers Dry Lake.

80
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:56,740
Edwards today includes nearly 5,000 active duty personnel,

81
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:00,980
8,000 family members, and 3,400 civilians.

82
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:04,700
The base has three elementary schools and a secondary school,

83
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:08,300
as well as a golf course, riding stables, a bowling alley,

84
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:13,800
and a movie theater. Edwards has come a long way

85
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:17,940
from its early days as a remote desert outpost known as Muroc.

86
00:05:17,941 --> 00:05:21,940
It got its name from a couple who settled in the area in 1910.

87
00:05:21,941 --> 00:05:23,940
But their name wasn't Muroc.

88
00:05:23,941 --> 00:05:25,940
It was Corum.

89
00:05:25,941 --> 00:05:28,940
Clifford and Effie Corum reversed the letters in their last name

90
00:05:28,941 --> 00:05:30,940
and called their new community Muroc,

91
00:05:30,941 --> 00:05:36,940
after discovering California already had a town called Corum.

92
00:05:36,941 --> 00:05:41,940
In 1933, the military arrived at Muroc and changed it forever.

93
00:05:41,941 --> 00:05:44,940
Lieutenant Colonel Hap Arnold of the U.S. Army Air Corps,

94
00:05:44,941 --> 00:05:46,940
commander of nearby March Field,

95
00:05:46,941 --> 00:05:50,940
was looking for a place to train bomber and fighter crews.

96
00:05:50,941 --> 00:05:54,940
He saw this enormous, flat expanse of dry lake bed.

97
00:05:54,941 --> 00:05:57,940
In his eyes, it was a natural aerodrome.

98
00:05:57,941 --> 00:06:01,940
So he sent up a crew here September of 1933,

99
00:06:01,941 --> 00:06:04,940
and they laid out a bombing and gunnery range

100
00:06:04,941 --> 00:06:06,940
along the east shore of the lake bed,

101
00:06:06,941 --> 00:06:09,940
right along that ridge up there.

102
00:06:09,941 --> 00:06:12,940
These guys, local settlers, felt sorry for them

103
00:06:12,941 --> 00:06:15,840
because they were stuck literally in the middle of nowhere.

104
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:18,840
The locals called them the Foreign Legion of the Army Air

105
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:19,940
Corps.

106
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:22,780
They often thought of this place as the hot Siberia of the Army

107
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:24,180
Air Corps.

108
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:26,880
Someone else arrived at Murak in the 1930s,

109
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,180
perhaps not so important as the Air Corps,

110
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:31,900
but a force to be reckoned with nonetheless.

111
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,100
Her name was Florence Poncho Barnes.

112
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:37,540
A wealthy socialite and pilot, she

113
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:40,040
set speed records for women aviators that rivaled those

114
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:41,640
of Amelia Earhart.

115
00:06:41,641 --> 00:06:44,440
She also became Hollywood's first female stunt pilot.

116
00:06:44,540 --> 00:06:47,840
She flew a stunt plane in Hell's Angels.

117
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:49,340
She knew Howard Hughes.

118
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:52,780
She went out with Raymond Navarro.

119
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:55,880
She was well-connected and had an awfully good time.

120
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:58,400
Poncho was born in a fantastic mansion,

121
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:01,600
had all kinds of money, had all kinds of social position,

122
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:04,100
all of which she lost during the Depression.

123
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:06,900
She had married a pastor in Pasadena,

124
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,100
a pastor of a very upscale Episcopal church.

125
00:07:10,101 --> 00:07:14,540
And no sooner had she become the wife of the Reverend Mr. Barnes

126
00:07:14,541 --> 00:07:19,740
than she began running weapons for Mexican revolutionaries.

127
00:07:19,741 --> 00:07:24,140
In 1935, Barnes traded some property for an alfalfa farm.

128
00:07:24,141 --> 00:07:27,140
She soon began supplying the base with fresh milk and pork

129
00:07:27,141 --> 00:07:32,460
from her farm east of Muroc. Later, Barnes would build a

130
00:07:32,461 --> 00:07:35,460
restaurant and bar on her property, which would become a

131
00:07:35,461 --> 00:07:39,460
legendary hangout for test pilots.

132
00:07:39,461 --> 00:07:45,060
In 1941, after the U.S. entered World War II, Muroch Bombing and

133
00:07:45,061 --> 00:07:50,060
Gunnery Range was renamed Muroch Army Air Base, and many pilots

134
00:07:50,061 --> 00:07:52,700
destined for combat were trained there.

135
00:07:52,701 --> 00:07:56,220
The base also took on a new role, flight testing.

136
00:07:56,221 --> 00:07:59,020
It would turn out to be Muroch's true calling.

137
00:07:59,021 --> 00:08:01,820
General Albert Boyd, who many consider the father of modern

138
00:08:01,821 --> 00:08:05,420
flight testing, called this place God's gift to the U.S.

139
00:08:05,421 --> 00:08:08,260
Air Force, and meaning specifically that lake bed.

140
00:08:08,261 --> 00:08:12,260
previously, most military planes had been flight tested at Wright

141
00:08:12,261 --> 00:08:16,260
Field in Ohio, but Wright was overloaded because of the war

142
00:08:16,261 --> 00:08:19,260
effort. A crucial new top secret project

143
00:08:19,261 --> 00:08:23,260
was going to need a more remote location and better weather to

144
00:08:23,261 --> 00:08:27,260
ensure rapid development. At a remote spot on the north

145
00:08:27,261 --> 00:08:30,260
shore of Rogers Dry Lake, the Army Air Corps built a new test

146
00:08:30,261 --> 00:08:35,260
site for America's first jet plane, the Bell XP-59A Aero

147
00:08:35,261 --> 00:08:41,400
era comet. It first flew in 1942,

148
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:44,900
but it was kept secret, even from the rest of the base.

149
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,500
These were airplane people.

150
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:49,100
They'd see an airplane sitting out here every day on the ramp,

151
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:52,420
perhaps, with no prop, and they'd begin to ask questions.

152
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:54,540
For that reason, they actually just

153
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:58,480
built a small dummy prop that they affixed to the nose of the

154
00:08:58,580 --> 00:09:00,520
P-59 whenever it was out here on the ramp.

155
00:09:00,521 --> 00:09:06,520
Now called North Base, the area is abandoned,

156
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:08,520
surrounded by tumbleweed and visited

157
00:09:08,620 --> 00:09:11,160
by the occasional coyote.

158
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:13,600
The hangar, as you can see, right on the edge

159
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:16,500
of the lake bed so that they could taxi the airplane right

160
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:19,100
out and take off any time they needed to,

161
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:21,400
going almost any direction.

162
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:23,740
The Bell-era comet was soon followed

163
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:27,940
by a second jet, the Lockheed XP-80 Shooting Star, which

164
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:29,380
turned out to be a much more successful

165
00:09:29,381 --> 00:09:31,880
to be a much more successful aircraft.

166
00:09:31,881 --> 00:09:34,380
In the words of Larry Bell of Bell Aircraft,

167
00:09:34,381 --> 00:09:37,720
it's nice to be first, but the money is in being second.

168
00:09:37,721 --> 00:09:40,860
A little-known maintenance officer from Wright Field

169
00:09:40,861 --> 00:09:45,160
came out here for the first time, September 1945.

170
00:09:45,161 --> 00:09:47,600
And he came out to be part of the team that

171
00:09:47,601 --> 00:09:49,840
conducted the accelerated service tests

172
00:09:49,841 --> 00:09:52,600
on the new P-80 shooting star.

173
00:09:52,601 --> 00:09:55,300
His name was Captain Chuck Yeager.

174
00:09:55,301 --> 00:09:57,500
When Chuck Yeager first got here,

175
00:09:57,501 --> 00:10:02,740
He said the place looked like the ass end of the moon to him.

176
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:06,180
Bob Hoover arrived at Murrock at around the same time,

177
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:08,980
and he and Yeager soon began testing new Air Force planes

178
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:10,320
at the base.

179
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:15,180
You had the opportunity of safety up there

180
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:17,980
because of that dry lake bed.

181
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:20,460
And it saved my neck two or three times.

182
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:25,700
On one occasion, I touched down at 240 knots

183
00:10:25,701 --> 00:10:29,700
and the landing gear and wheels had never been up to that kind

184
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:33,200
of speed before. And it was one of the smoothest

185
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:35,540
landings I've ever ridden through.

186
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:39,740
But I rode 11 miles across that lake bed.

187
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:42,660
One of the most exciting eras in aviation history,

188
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:46,160
the turbojet revolution, ushered in the golden age of flight

189
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:49,760
testing and gave birth to an archetypal image, that of the

190
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:54,200
dashing and daring test pilot. One of them, Chuck Yeager, was

191
00:10:54,201 --> 00:10:57,560
about to do what some considered impossible.

192
00:10:57,561 --> 00:11:01,640
Approximately 10,000 years ago, Lake Thompson, an Ice Age

193
00:11:01,641 --> 00:11:04,760
remnant, dried up. Rogers Dry Lake is part of its

194
00:11:04,761 --> 00:11:07,640
ancient lake bed. Edwards Air Force Base will

195
00:11:07,641 --> 00:11:15,160
return on Modern Marvels. We now return to Edwards Air

196
00:11:15,161 --> 00:11:21,240
Force Base on Modern Marvels. The turbojet revolution was

197
00:11:21,241 --> 00:11:24,840
revolution was the first of many revolutions at Muroc, each made

198
00:11:24,841 --> 00:11:29,480
possible or made necessary by its predecessor.

199
00:11:29,481 --> 00:11:33,640
After the turbojet revolution came the supersonic revolution.

200
00:11:33,641 --> 00:11:37,400
The rocket-powered Bell X-1 was the first in a long series of

201
00:11:37,401 --> 00:11:41,040
research airplanes designed to explore the unknown frontier

202
00:11:41,041 --> 00:11:44,640
beyond the speed of sound. There had been speculation

203
00:11:44,641 --> 00:11:47,080
about whether it was even possible for an airplane to break

204
00:11:47,081 --> 00:11:51,420
the so-called sound barrier. Some scientists warned that G

205
00:11:51,421 --> 00:11:54,740
forces might become infinite at the speed of sound and destroy

206
00:11:54,741 --> 00:11:57,240
the aircraft. The rocket engine that would

207
00:11:57,241 --> 00:12:00,680
propel the X-1 was also full of unknowns.

208
00:12:00,681 --> 00:12:04,680
When the X-1 program came along, rocket motors were, of course,

209
00:12:04,681 --> 00:12:08,940
a very new idea. A lot of unknown quantities,

210
00:12:08,941 --> 00:12:11,080
and they tended to blow up occasionally.

211
00:12:11,081 --> 00:12:14,080
So what was needed was a secluded area where you could

212
00:12:14,081 --> 00:12:16,080
the engines and monitor them.

213
00:12:16,180 --> 00:12:18,880
What could be better than one of the revetments?

214
00:12:18,980 --> 00:12:20,820
Adobe structures called revetments

215
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:22,760
were built in the mid-40s at the air base

216
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:25,520
as experimental field shelters for aircraft.

217
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:28,260
The X-1 rocket engine was tested at this one

218
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:30,900
on the south side of Rogers Dry Lake.

219
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,060
We're standing right now in the middle of the rocket engine

220
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:37,340
test stand, which was built in the middle of this B-29

221
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:38,440
revetment.

222
00:12:38,540 --> 00:12:40,080
Over here, you can see the blockhouse.

223
00:12:40,180 --> 00:12:44,080
It's a very simple affair with a thick armored glass window.

224
00:12:44,081 --> 00:12:48,020
In here, the project engineers, a couple of scientists,

225
00:12:48,021 --> 00:12:51,580
the managers would be standing protected watching the rocket

226
00:12:51,581 --> 00:12:52,680
motor run.

227
00:12:52,681 --> 00:12:55,280
The rocket engine would have been suspended about where I'm

228
00:12:55,281 --> 00:12:58,680
standing with the head end of the rocket pointed toward the

229
00:12:58,681 --> 00:13:03,720
adobe wall and the nozzle end pointed out this way so that the

230
00:13:03,721 --> 00:13:07,440
rocket blast would roll harmless layout across the desert and

231
00:13:07,441 --> 00:13:09,480
into the lake bed.

232
00:13:09,481 --> 00:13:12,480
Beginning with the X-1 project, most purely experimental

233
00:13:12,481 --> 00:13:16,080
would be conducted jointly by two government agencies,

234
00:13:16,180 --> 00:13:19,820
one military and the other civilian.

235
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:22,120
It's always thought of as an Air Force base,

236
00:13:22,220 --> 00:13:23,520
and indeed it is.

237
00:13:23,620 --> 00:13:26,920
But it also has had another component.

238
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:29,840
It had a group from the National Advisory Committee

239
00:13:29,940 --> 00:13:32,140
for Aeronautics, or the NACA.

240
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:37,440
The small group of six arrived here in September 1946.

241
00:13:37,540 --> 00:13:42,480
And they came out because of the famous X-1 program.

242
00:13:42,481 --> 00:13:45,880
Decades later, the NACA changed its name.

243
00:13:45,881 --> 00:13:48,620
We know it today as NASA.

244
00:13:48,621 --> 00:13:52,820
But whatever the project that the airplane was designed for,

245
00:13:52,821 --> 00:13:57,320
it would be first flown by the Air Force, who would test it,

246
00:13:57,321 --> 00:14:00,440
make sure it was safe, and they would fly the airplane out to

247
00:14:00,441 --> 00:14:02,940
the limits of its performance.

248
00:14:02,941 --> 00:14:06,340
And then it would be turned over to NACA, which was located

249
00:14:06,341 --> 00:14:08,580
right here at Edwards Air Force Base.

250
00:14:08,581 --> 00:14:11,080
In the 1950s, most record-breaking flights were

