00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:35,340
exhaust of the rocket motors. I knew he had accomplished this

289
00:16:35,341 --> 00:16:38,100
mission. So on the way down, I said,

290
00:16:38,101 --> 00:16:40,740
Pard, I think you're going to get that free steak dinner

291
00:16:40,741 --> 00:16:43,480
tonight. Yeager got his free steak

292
00:16:43,481 --> 00:16:47,020
at Poncho's, but the celebration was cut short when an officer

293
00:16:47,021 --> 00:16:50,360
arrived from headquarters. We hadn't any more than

294
00:16:50,361 --> 00:16:54,860
been there maybe 30 or 40 minutes, and somebody came in and said,

295
00:16:54,861 --> 00:17:01,860
it's classified as top secret. We're not to talk about it.

296
00:17:01,861 --> 00:17:10,620
The details of that day remained largely unknown to the public until a few decades later,

297
00:17:10,621 --> 00:17:16,060
when author Tom Wolfe wrote a book called The Right Stuff. Published in 1979, it was

298
00:17:16,061 --> 00:17:19,560
turned into a movie that won four Academy Awards in 1983.

299
00:17:19,561 --> 00:17:22,560
Tom Wolfe, The Right Stuff, The Right Stuff, The Right Stuff, The Right Stuff, The Right

300
00:17:22,561 --> 00:17:23,560
Stuff.

301
00:17:23,561 --> 00:17:26,560
Tom Wolfe, The Right Stuff, The Right Stuff, The Right Stuff, The Right Stuff, The Right

302
00:17:26,561 --> 00:17:27,560
Stuff.

303
00:17:27,561 --> 00:17:31,300
He was not known at all.

304
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:33,560
I had never heard of Chuck Yeager when I went to Edwards.

305
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:39,900
I was just there to see how the astronauts had trained.

306
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,700
And in a way, Yeager took over my book

307
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:47,820
because his exploits were more in a kind of primitive way,

308
00:17:47,920 --> 00:17:51,220
much more daring than anything that the astronauts did,

309
00:17:51,320 --> 00:17:52,420
or more colorful.

310
00:17:52,520 --> 00:17:55,920
In November of 1953, the good times at Poncho's

311
00:17:55,921 --> 00:17:59,320
came to an end when a restaurant and motel mysteriously burned to

312
00:17:59,321 --> 00:18:03,200
the ground. Today, as planes like the

313
00:18:03,201 --> 00:18:06,560
Airborne Laser are flight tested overhead...

314
00:18:06,561 --> 00:18:10,160
That's it all right. With this chase plane.

315
00:18:10,161 --> 00:18:14,400
The burned-out ruins of Poncho Barnes Place remain below.

316
00:18:14,401 --> 00:18:18,240
Here we are in the remains of the past, and the future is

317
00:18:18,241 --> 00:18:21,440
taking off right over our heads with the Airborne Laser.

318
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:32,920
Good times and bad times populate the past at Edwards, and some of its memories are buried

319
00:18:32,921 --> 00:18:37,920
in its soil. Peter Merlin is hunting for history.

320
00:18:37,921 --> 00:18:41,349
This is the crash site of the YB-49 flying wing.

321
00:18:41,350 --> 00:18:43,920
They were testing control forces and

322
00:18:43,921 --> 00:18:48,920
apparently overstressed the airframe, broke off the outer wing panels, and the plane crashed

323
00:18:48,921 --> 00:18:52,920
right behind us. An aviation researcher who does

324
00:18:52,921 --> 00:18:57,160
archiving at Edwards, Merlin calls his hobby aviation

325
00:18:57,161 --> 00:19:02,360
archaeology. Some say the revolutionary YB-49

326
00:19:02,361 --> 00:19:06,160
flying-wing bomber that crashed here was too far ahead of its

327
00:19:06,161 --> 00:19:09,680
time. This piece here is part of a

328
00:19:09,681 --> 00:19:14,680
hinge line with some structure and skin of the aircraft.

329
00:19:14,681 --> 00:19:20,680
It's a good-sized piece. Here's part of the plexiglass

330
00:19:20,681 --> 00:19:22,480
from the windows.

331
00:19:22,580 --> 00:19:24,920
It was an eight-inch and jet bomber.

332
00:19:25,020 --> 00:19:27,880
There was a lot of fuel on board, so it was quite a fire.

333
00:19:30,820 --> 00:19:32,720
Without the computer-controlled flight

334
00:19:32,820 --> 00:19:35,300
systems of today's modern fighters and bombers,

335
00:19:35,400 --> 00:19:37,300
the flying wing had limited stability

336
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:38,600
and was difficult to control.

337
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:43,740
This is quite a screen this is making.

338
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:45,640
I think I may do a little digging here.

339
00:19:45,641 --> 00:19:47,640
digging here.

340
00:19:55,640 --> 00:19:59,640
I got a piece of rusty steel.

341
00:19:59,641 --> 00:20:03,640
There's a bolt through it and a nut.

342
00:20:03,641 --> 00:20:07,640
It's very corroded but heavy.

343
00:20:09,640 --> 00:20:13,640
Hard to say what it was.

344
00:20:13,641 --> 00:20:17,640
This crash gave Edwards Air Force Base its name.

345
00:20:17,740 --> 00:20:20,640
Captain Glenn Edwards and four other men were killed in the

346
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:25,080
mishap, and a year later, in December 1949, Murak Air Force

347
00:20:25,180 --> 00:20:29,080
Base was renamed Edwards Air Force Base in honor of Captain

348
00:20:29,180 --> 00:20:31,600
Edwards. It wasn't the first tragedy at the

349
00:20:31,700 --> 00:20:34,100
base, and it wouldn't be the last.

350
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:37,100
No one knew better than the test pilots that the job had its

351
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:40,600
risks. But in spite of the dangers and

352
00:20:40,601 --> 00:20:43,500
setbacks, a golden age of aeronautical research would

353
00:20:43,501 --> 00:20:47,800
come to Edwards Air Force Base in the 1950s.

354
00:20:47,801 --> 00:20:50,840
Experimental rocket planes continued to raise the bar for

355
00:20:50,841 --> 00:20:54,340
speed and altitude. Chuck Yeager set a new record in

356
00:20:54,341 --> 00:20:59,340
1953 as he piloted the second generation Bell X-1A to a speed

357
00:20:59,341 --> 00:21:04,760
of Mach 2.44, or 1,650 miles per hour.

358
00:21:04,761 --> 00:21:08,140
Yeager's record was broken less than three years later when

359
00:21:08,141 --> 00:21:12,080
when Captain Mel Apt in the Bell X-2 became the first man to fly

360
00:21:12,081 --> 00:21:16,040
faster than Mach 3. For about a minute and a half,

361
00:21:16,041 --> 00:21:18,380
he was the fastest man in the world.

362
00:21:18,381 --> 00:21:21,980
At that point, he tried to slow down and turn back toward the

363
00:21:21,981 --> 00:21:25,080
lake bed to glide in and make his landing there, just like the

364
00:21:25,081 --> 00:21:29,500
X-1. Seconds after hitting Mach 3.2,

365
00:21:29,501 --> 00:21:32,900
the X-2 rocket plane tumbled violently out of control and

366
00:21:32,901 --> 00:21:36,400
crashed in the desert near the base. It crashed over here at

367
00:21:36,401 --> 00:21:40,000
at the lip of the sandy wash and broke into several pieces.

368
00:21:40,780 --> 00:21:43,280
The engine section was right over here.

369
00:21:43,740 --> 00:21:45,800
The mid-fuselage assembly and wings

370
00:21:45,801 --> 00:21:48,280
and the forward fuselage lay over there.

371
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:51,540
We've got a piece of the skin of the airplane.

372
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:54,460
You can see it's still got the white paint on it.

373
00:21:56,660 --> 00:22:00,300
To continue developing faster and safer military aircraft,

374
00:22:00,960 --> 00:22:03,700
Edwards flight researchers would need to process reams

375
00:22:03,701 --> 00:22:05,540
of complex data from test flights.

376
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:10,900
computers, Edwards would have to find a unique solution.

377
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:14,640
In May of 1953, in a flight that began at Edwards, civilian

378
00:22:14,740 --> 00:22:17,540
pilot Jacqueline Cochran became the first woman to exceed the

379
00:22:17,640 --> 00:22:20,240
speed of sound. Her chase pilot was Chuck

380
00:22:20,340 --> 00:22:22,440
Yeager. Edwards Air Force Base will

381
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:28,360
return. We now return to Edwards Air Force

382
00:22:28,460 --> 00:22:34,300
Base on Modern Marvels. In the early days of flight

383
00:22:34,301 --> 00:22:37,300
testing at Edwards, some of the most difficult work involved the

384
00:22:37,301 --> 00:22:41,300
detailed analysis of complex flight data.

385
00:22:41,301 --> 00:22:46,300
In 1952, Betty Love joined a team of women called Computers,

386
00:22:46,301 --> 00:22:49,300
who booted up and crunched the numbers every day.

387
00:22:49,301 --> 00:22:53,300
When I went to work, we were issued a six-inch film scale,

388
00:22:53,301 --> 00:22:56,300
a light box. We were given a triangle, a

389
00:22:56,301 --> 00:23:01,300
pointer to be used to read the calibration, and we were given

390
00:23:01,301 --> 00:23:04,640
given certain pencils, 2H pencils to use.

391
00:23:04,740 --> 00:23:07,840
The women who came here were known as computers because,

392
00:23:07,940 --> 00:23:09,340
very simply, they computed.

393
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:12,340
They computed data from these film strips.

394
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:13,440
And it was quite an art.

395
00:23:13,540 --> 00:23:19,860
It was done in an office here in which silence was golden.

396
00:23:19,960 --> 00:23:23,760
Noise wasn't, conversation was not tolerated.

397
00:23:23,860 --> 00:23:26,260
Flight information was recorded aboard test planes

398
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:27,960
on rolls of film.

399
00:23:28,060 --> 00:23:30,700
The women known as computers turned the film into tables

400
00:23:30,701 --> 00:23:36,100
graphs for the engineers. I have a VGH recorder here that

401
00:23:36,101 --> 00:23:42,100
recorded airspeed, altitude, and lateral and vertical

402
00:23:42,101 --> 00:23:47,100
acceleration. And on this particular flight, we were

403
00:23:47,101 --> 00:23:52,600
