489
00:28:49,881 --> 00:28:51,880
The legendary Blackbirds were used for high-altitude

490
00:28:51,881 --> 00:28:55,880
reconnaissance by the Air Force until their role was taken over

491
00:28:55,881 --> 00:28:59,880
by satellites in the 1990s. But their speed record still

492
00:28:59,881 --> 00:29:02,880
stands. The Blackbirds are faster than

493
00:29:02,881 --> 00:29:07,880
any known manned jet aircraft. But even a Blackbird would look

494
00:29:07,881 --> 00:29:10,640
compared to the space shuttle, which would evolve from an

495
00:29:10,641 --> 00:29:14,340
unusual series of wingless aircraft soon to be tested at

496
00:29:14,341 --> 00:29:17,220
Edwards. One of these ungainly airplanes

497
00:29:17,221 --> 00:29:21,540
would be dubbed the Flying Bathtub.

498
00:29:21,541 --> 00:29:25,120
In a single stop, the brakes of the XB-70 Valkyrie

499
00:29:25,121 --> 00:29:29,120
absorbed the kinetic energy it takes to stop 800 automobiles

500
00:29:29,121 --> 00:29:33,120
from a speed of 100 miles per hour.

501
00:29:33,121 --> 00:29:37,120
Edwards Air Force Base will return on Modern Marvels.

502
00:29:37,880 --> 00:29:39,880
We now return to Edwards Air Force Base

503
00:29:39,980 --> 00:29:41,880
on Modern Marvels.

504
00:29:45,880 --> 00:29:47,880
A group of strange looking aircraft began to streak

505
00:29:47,980 --> 00:29:50,880
through the skies above Edwards in the mid-60s.

506
00:29:50,980 --> 00:29:53,880
They were called heavyweight lifting bodies and helped pave

507
00:29:53,980 --> 00:29:55,880
the way for the space shuttle.

508
00:29:55,980 --> 00:29:58,880
These wingless aircraft relied on the fuselage alone

509
00:29:58,980 --> 00:30:00,880
to create lift.

510
00:30:00,980 --> 00:30:04,880
I went straight from the cockpit of the X-15

511
00:30:04,881 --> 00:30:10,580
X-15 to the HL-10, which was one of four heavyweight lifting

512
00:30:10,581 --> 00:30:13,080
bodies that NASA and the Air Force tested.

513
00:30:13,081 --> 00:30:17,120
They flew a lot better than they looked like they should.

514
00:30:17,121 --> 00:30:19,700
The lifting bodies proved that the shuttle would not need

515
00:30:19,701 --> 00:30:24,300
onboard jet engines to land, and in fact, could land dead stick,

516
00:30:24,301 --> 00:30:27,640
that is, without power. NASA had originally planned to

517
00:30:27,641 --> 00:30:30,640
have as many as four jet engines onboard the shuttle.

518
00:30:30,641 --> 00:30:34,640
Approve. One. Touchdown.

519
00:30:34,641 --> 00:30:38,640
They took the jet engines out of the space shuttle, and that

520
00:30:38,641 --> 00:30:43,640
resulted in a savings of a million pounds of launch takeoff

521
00:30:43,641 --> 00:30:46,640
weight. In the early 70s, a new

522
00:30:46,641 --> 00:30:49,640
technological revolution would transform flight testing at

523
00:30:49,641 --> 00:30:53,640
Edwards. It was the systems revolution, and it was brought

524
00:30:53,641 --> 00:30:57,640
on by advancements in avionics. The word avionics, a

525
00:30:57,641 --> 00:31:00,940
a contraction of aviation and electronics, was coined to

526
00:31:00,941 --> 00:31:04,940
describe electronic systems used in aircraft.

527
00:31:04,941 --> 00:31:07,940
Progress in avionics would allow the development of so-called

528
00:31:07,941 --> 00:31:10,940
fly-by-wire flight control systems at Edwards, which used

529
00:31:10,941 --> 00:31:15,440
computers to replace conventional mechanical controls.

530
00:31:15,441 --> 00:31:19,440
In the late 1970s, at a secret location, the Edwards Flight

531
00:31:19,441 --> 00:31:22,440
Test Center was leading the way in yet another revolution.

532
00:31:22,441 --> 00:31:26,440
It was the stealth revolution. An Edwards test pilot and a pair

533
00:31:26,441 --> 00:31:29,240
of flight test engineers were testing Lockheed's low

534
00:31:29,340 --> 00:31:32,440
observable technology demonstrator, code named

535
00:31:32,540 --> 00:31:36,440
HALF BLUE. The test program conducted jointly

536
00:31:36,540 --> 00:31:39,980
by the Edwards Flight Test Center and Lockheed soon led to a

537
00:31:40,080 --> 00:31:45,300
new attack aircraft, the F-117A Nighthawk, also known as the

538
00:31:45,400 --> 00:31:56,440
Stealth Fighter. Edwards Air Force Base also played

539
00:31:56,441 --> 00:31:59,280
in flight testing the first space shuttle, which first took

540
00:31:59,380 --> 00:32:03,280
to the skies on top of a modified 747 called the shuttle

541
00:32:03,380 --> 00:32:04,980
carrier.

542
00:32:05,080 --> 00:32:07,580
Fitz Fulton flew the shuttle carrier on a number of missions.

543
00:32:07,680 --> 00:32:09,380
It handles much better without the shuttle,

544
00:32:09,480 --> 00:32:11,980
but with the shuttle, it still flies pretty good.

545
00:32:12,080 --> 00:32:14,500
The speed of that airplane with the shuttle on top

546
00:32:14,600 --> 00:32:17,200
is about 2 thirds of the speed of a standard airplane,

547
00:32:17,300 --> 00:32:20,100
and it uses twice the amount of fuel per hour.

548
00:32:20,200 --> 00:32:22,740
The shuttle first separated from the 747

549
00:32:22,840 --> 00:32:25,960
and glided to a test landing at Edwards in August

550
00:32:25,961 --> 00:32:29,960
in August of 1977. Then, after more than a decade

551
00:32:29,961 --> 00:32:35,060
of research and testing, on April 14, 1981, the wheels of the space

552
00:32:35,061 --> 00:32:39,060
shuttle Columbia touched down on Rogers Dry Lakebed after its first

553
00:32:39,061 --> 00:32:41,700
space mission. It was the first orbiting space

554
00:32:41,701 --> 00:32:45,320
vehicle ever to leave the Earth on rocket power and fly back to Earth

555
00:32:45,321 --> 00:32:48,260
on wings like an airplane.

556
00:32:48,261 --> 00:32:55,260
In the 1980s, as the complexity of avionics increased rapidly,

557
00:32:55,261 --> 00:32:58,260
new systems were needed to handle huge amounts of

558
00:32:58,261 --> 00:33:02,260
experimental flight data. New facilities were created at

559
00:33:02,261 --> 00:33:06,260
Edwards to allow more testing of software-intensive aircraft

560
00:33:06,261 --> 00:33:10,260
systems on the ground. One of them was the Modeling and

561
00:33:10,261 --> 00:33:14,260
Simulation Facility, located on the west side of Rogers Dry

562
00:33:14,261 --> 00:33:16,260
Lake. What we do here in the

563
00:33:16,261 --> 00:33:21,300
simulation facility is we want to basically test the scenario

564
00:33:21,301 --> 00:33:24,340
before we actually go fly. So early in the development program,

565
00:33:24,341 --> 00:33:26,660
we'll get our engineers involved with the development of a system.

566
00:33:26,661 --> 00:33:29,300
They'll learn as much as they can about it, and they'll bring that

567
00:33:29,301 --> 00:33:34,100
information to a facility like this to actually fly an airplane in a

568
00:33:34,101 --> 00:33:37,700
realistic environment, but a model and simulation environment, but as

569
00:33:37,701 --> 00:33:42,340
realistic as we can nevertheless. The system can be configured to

570
00:33:42,341 --> 00:33:45,780
emulate any aircraft, including planes that haven't even been built

571
00:33:45,781 --> 00:33:47,780
yet.

572
00:33:47,880 --> 00:33:50,280
The next level of ground testing at Edwards is called

573
00:33:50,380 --> 00:33:52,180
hardware-in-the-loop testing.

574
00:33:52,280 --> 00:33:55,680
We'll take actual hardware from our system.

575
00:33:55,780 --> 00:33:58,320
For example, we have an F-16 airplane duplicated on a bench

576
00:33:58,420 --> 00:33:59,320
upstairs.

577
00:33:59,420 --> 00:34:01,820
We'll take the actual hardware, the radar, the avionics, and

578
00:34:01,920 --> 00:34:04,640
put them on a bench with the actual cables, because cable

579
00:34:04,740 --> 00:34:06,640
length quite often is very important.

580
00:34:06,740 --> 00:34:09,680
And we will replicate flying that airplane on the ground

581
00:34:09,780 --> 00:34:12,080
using the actual hardware.

582
00:34:12,180 --> 00:34:15,380
And over here, we have some of the avionics computers that go

583
00:34:15,381 --> 00:34:16,380
on board the F-16.

584
00:34:16,381 --> 00:34:20,480
We've got the general avionics computer that is one of the main computers for the actual

585
00:34:20,481 --> 00:34:22,560
avionics on board the aircraft.

586
00:34:22,561 --> 00:34:27,540
It's also connected to the fire control radar that is one of the main computers for the

587
00:34:27,541 --> 00:34:30,480
actual fire control radar, the F-16 radar.

588
00:34:30,481 --> 00:34:34,900
We have more of the F-16 radar here, the boxes for the transmitter.

589
00:34:34,901 --> 00:34:37,620
So these are all parts of the radar that go inside the F-16.

590
00:34:37,621 --> 00:34:42,060
And they all couple with the actual antenna, which is behind that bulkhead right here.

591
00:34:42,061 --> 00:34:45,100
So behind the bulkhead is an F-16 antenna that'll sweep when

592
00:34:45,101 --> 00:34:47,460
we're doing a test, going back and forth sweeping.

593
00:34:47,461 --> 00:34:49,500
And it's looking out over the dry lake bed, the Edwards dry

594
00:34:49,501 --> 00:34:50,500
lake beds.

595
00:34:50,501 --> 00:34:52,940
A lot of our tests are really about integration of old

596
00:34:52,941 --> 00:34:56,140
weapons into the airplane with new software loads.

597
00:34:56,141 --> 00:34:58,480
This particular one right here is a Maverick.

598
00:34:58,481 --> 00:35:00,680
It's an infrared version of a Maverick missile.

599
00:35:00,681 --> 00:35:03,820
So when we do tests with this, we're just making sure that the

600
00:35:03,821 --> 00:35:07,020
Maverick missile integrates correctly with the new software

601
00:35:07,021 --> 00:35:08,920
loads from the F-16.

602
00:35:08,921 --> 00:35:12,060
Right next door is the Benefield Anechoic facility.

603
00:35:12,061 --> 00:35:14,860
the largest of its kind in the world.

604
00:35:14,861 --> 00:35:18,140
It's used to test electronic systems, including communications

605
00:35:18,141 --> 00:35:21,400
and radar, in an environment free of radio interference

606
00:35:21,401 --> 00:35:25,420
or reflections that is similar to the environment found in flight.

607
00:35:25,421 --> 00:35:29,300
Dictionary says anechoic is free from echo or free from reflection.

608
00:35:29,301 --> 00:35:31,420
So once we're inside that chamber,

609
00:35:31,421 --> 00:35:33,860
all the electromagnetic signals, the radar signals,

610
00:35:33,861 --> 00:35:36,020
the radio signals don't bounce off the walls.

611
00:35:36,021 --> 00:35:38,060
Because we have the walls completely aligned with what we

612
00:35:38,061 --> 00:35:40,460
call RAM, or radar absorbent material.

613
00:35:40,461 --> 00:35:43,400
There's over 51,000 pieces of this ram,

614
00:35:43,480 --> 00:35:46,120
which is a carbon-impregnated fiber foam

615
00:35:46,121 --> 00:35:50,420
that absorbs the radar and radio waves

616
00:35:50,421 --> 00:35:52,100
as they are in the chamber.

617
00:35:52,940 --> 00:35:56,560
It's about 260 feet by 250 feet by 70 feet high

618
00:35:56,561 --> 00:35:57,720
on the inside of the chamber.

619
00:35:58,180 --> 00:36:00,940
We can hang any fighter-sized aircraft

620
00:36:00,941 --> 00:36:02,940
from any one of two hoists in the chamber.

621
00:36:03,420 --> 00:36:05,780
We have an 80-foot diameter turntable

622
00:36:05,781 --> 00:36:07,940
in the middle of the chamber we can rotate aircraft on.

623
00:36:07,941 --> 00:36:11,940
As high-tech developments in avionics continued in the 1980s,

624
00:36:11,941 --> 00:36:14,940
they helped bring a familiar sight back to Edwards Air

625
00:36:14,941 --> 00:36:17,940
Force Base. Large flying wings would be in the

626
00:36:17,941 --> 00:36:20,940
skies above Rogers Dry Lake for the first time in nearly 40

627
00:36:20,941 --> 00:36:23,940
years. But this time, they would have

628
00:36:23,941 --> 00:36:27,940
computerized flight controllers to keep them in the air.

629
00:36:27,941 --> 00:36:30,940
The door of the Benefield Anechoic Chamber is the largest

630
00:36:30,941 --> 00:36:34,940
single-piece door in the world and takes 45 minutes to open

631
00:36:34,941 --> 00:36:38,700
minutes to open or close. Edwards Air Force Base will

632
00:36:38,701 --> 00:36:46,840
return on Modern Marvels. We now return to Edwards Air

633
00:36:46,841 --> 00:36:53,420
Force Base on Modern Marvels. Although the original Northrop

634
00:36:53,421 --> 00:36:57,300
flying wing was a failure at Edwards in the 1940s, new

635
00:36:57,301 --> 00:37:00,300
developments in avionics have made the flying wing design more

636
00:37:00,301 --> 00:37:05,400
viable, and Northrop tried again, this time successfully.

637
00:37:05,401 --> 00:37:08,300
The new flying wing bomber, first flight tested at Edwards

638
00:37:08,301 --> 00:37:11,740
in the late 80s, has exactly the same wingspan as the first

639
00:37:11,741 --> 00:37:17,540
flying wing, 172 feet. The highly advanced B-2 bomber

640
00:37:17,541 --> 00:37:20,960
was designed almost entirely by computer.

641
00:37:20,961 --> 00:37:24,160
It has low observable characteristics that represent a

642
00:37:24,161 --> 00:37:26,760
third generation of stealth technology.

643
00:37:26,761 --> 00:37:33,560
generation SR-71 and second generation F-117 Nighthawk.

644
00:37:33,561 --> 00:37:37,000
After the B-2 stealth bomber, the next state-of-the-art combat

645
00:37:37,001 --> 00:37:41,640
aircraft to be tested at Edwards was the F-A-22 Raptor, a new

646
00:37:41,641 --> 00:37:44,680
air dominance fighter, which combined stealth with

647
00:37:44,681 --> 00:37:49,080
supersonic cruise ability. Its testing continues at Edwards.

648
00:37:49,081 --> 00:37:52,680
It is the greatest fighter plane ever, and I didn't start to

649
00:37:52,681 --> 00:37:56,760
realize exactly how much capability that went into it.

650
00:37:56,761 --> 00:37:59,700
until I started understanding some of the technical issues.

651
00:37:59,800 --> 00:38:02,060
There's nothing on the rafter that's there by accident,

652
00:38:02,160 --> 00:38:03,660
whether it was for radar reflections

653
00:38:03,760 --> 00:38:05,560
or whether it was for heat signature,

654
00:38:05,660 --> 00:38:08,200
or whether it was for top speed or loads,

655
00:38:08,300 --> 00:38:10,600
or all the different shapes serve a purpose.

656
00:38:10,700 --> 00:38:12,600
I think right now, any fighter pilot that's

657
00:38:12,700 --> 00:38:15,480
flying any other aircraft out there would be lying if he said

658
00:38:15,580 --> 00:38:17,180
he didn't want to fly it at least once.

659
00:38:17,280 --> 00:38:19,020
And after they fly it once, they'll be hooked.

660
00:38:19,120 --> 00:38:22,420
It's an awesome aircraft to fly.

661
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:24,920
In the northeast corner of Edwards Air Force Base

662
00:38:24,921 --> 00:38:28,760
complex of rocket test stands, where more reliable, more

663
00:38:28,761 --> 00:38:31,560
economical, and more powerful rocket engines are being

664
00:38:31,561 --> 00:38:34,600
developed for future space missions and for military

665
00:38:34,601 --> 00:38:38,040
applications. Test Stand 1A was originally designed

666
00:38:38,041 --> 00:38:42,040
and built more than 50 years ago to develop the rocket engine for

667
00:38:42,041 --> 00:38:45,000
the first intercontinental ballistic missile.

668
00:38:45,001 --> 00:38:48,280
The 65-square-mile rocket complex is operated by the Air

669
00:38:48,281 --> 00:38:53,280
Force Research Laboratory. Radical new ideas in jet-powered

670
00:38:53,281 --> 00:38:56,980
in jet-powered aircraft continue to be explored at Edwards.

671
00:38:56,981 --> 00:39:01,680
One of them is the $41 million Active Aeroelastic Wing, or

672
00:39:01,681 --> 00:39:05,780
AAW, project, being developed at the NASA Dryden Flight

673
00:39:05,781 --> 00:39:10,680
Research Center on the west side of Rogers Dry Lake.

674
00:39:10,681 --> 00:39:13,220
The AAW was a high-tech version of something the Wright

675
00:39:13,221 --> 00:39:16,580
brothers pioneered, using wing warping to control the flight

676
00:39:16,581 --> 00:39:19,140
of an aircraft. Orville Wright didn't have

677
00:39:19,141 --> 00:39:22,180
ailerons or flaps for control when he flew the first airplane

678
00:39:22,181 --> 00:39:23,880
in 1903.

679
00:39:23,881 --> 00:39:27,320
Lying in a saddle connected by cables to both wingtips,

680
00:39:27,321 --> 00:39:30,580
he steered the plane by rolling his hips from side to side

681
00:39:30,581 --> 00:39:33,120
and warping the wingtips up or down.

682
00:39:33,121 --> 00:39:36,060
With the Wright brothers' idea, which is to actually warp one

683
00:39:36,061 --> 00:39:40,500
wing up and one wing down, you can have a very flexible wing.

684
00:39:40,501 --> 00:39:44,540
And our technology is basically to apply the ideas of wing

685
00:39:44,541 --> 00:39:48,680
warping at very high speed on a supersonic jet.

686
00:39:48,681 --> 00:39:51,140
When I make an input that says I want to roll the airplane,

687
00:39:51,141 --> 00:39:53,740
let's say, to the left at a certain rate,

688
00:39:53,840 --> 00:39:55,740
the leading edge and trailing edge surfaces

689
00:39:55,840 --> 00:39:59,240
will deflect not to roll the airplane,

690
00:39:59,340 --> 00:40:02,520
but actually to twist the wing structure, if you will,

691
00:40:02,620 --> 00:40:04,880
like taking a towel and twisting it,

692
00:40:04,980 --> 00:40:07,120
such that as the wing structure twists,

693
00:40:07,220 --> 00:40:10,860
the lift generated by that wing will change,

694
00:40:10,960 --> 00:40:12,800
and the airplane will actually roll based

695
00:40:12,900 --> 00:40:15,560
on the twisted structure.

696
00:40:15,660 --> 00:40:17,600
Before flight testing at Edwards,

697
00:40:17,700 --> 00:40:21,140
the AAW's wings were modified by the Boeing Phantom Works.

698
00:40:21,141 --> 00:40:24,880
thin, flexible skin panels to allow warping.

699
00:40:24,980 --> 00:40:26,380
There was a lot of trepidation.

700
00:40:26,480 --> 00:40:28,680
One of the challenges of this type of technology

701
00:40:28,780 --> 00:40:33,180
is that you're introducing very high loads and unknown loads

702
00:40:33,280 --> 00:40:35,380
into the wing structure.

703
00:40:35,480 --> 00:40:37,760
Also at the NASA Research Center at Edwards,

704
00:40:37,860 --> 00:40:41,620
in the same hangar as the AAW, is the Pathfinder,

705
00:40:41,720 --> 00:40:45,200
a remotely piloted flying wing designed to stay aloft for long

706
00:40:45,300 --> 00:40:46,200
periods.

707
00:40:51,140 --> 00:40:54,880
With its solar power and 120-foot wingspan,

708
00:40:54,980 --> 00:40:58,020
it could conceivably stay in the air for weeks or even months

709
00:40:58,120 --> 00:41:00,020
at a time.

710
00:41:00,120 --> 00:41:02,760
A true flying wing design, the Pathfinder

711
00:41:02,860 --> 00:41:06,020
maneuvers without rudders, ailerons, or tail surfaces.

712
00:41:06,120 --> 00:41:09,260
Fittingly, it's being tested at the home of the flying wing,

713
00:41:09,360 --> 00:41:11,260
Edwards Air Force Base.

714
00:41:11,360 --> 00:41:15,460
What goes around comes around.

715
00:41:15,560 --> 00:41:18,100
The Pathfinder was first developed as a military plane

716
00:41:18,101 --> 00:41:21,000
for high-altitude, long-endurance surveillance.

717
00:41:21,001 --> 00:41:23,900
In the early 80s, it was originally developed under a

718
00:41:23,901 --> 00:41:27,140
classified program, and so nobody heard about it until

719
00:41:27,141 --> 00:41:31,740
about 1992, 1993. The military program was

720
00:41:31,741 --> 00:41:35,260
later canceled, and now the NASA Research Center at Edwards is

721
00:41:35,261 --> 00:41:38,060
exploring the pathfinder's potential for non-military

722
00:41:38,061 --> 00:41:41,260
uses. Some of the things they could

723
00:41:41,261 --> 00:41:44,460
do are in the area of agriculture, you could do

724
00:41:44,461 --> 00:41:49,760
large stretches of land to search out drought situations,

725
00:41:49,860 --> 00:41:52,860
flooding situations, disease situations.

726
00:41:52,960 --> 00:41:57,100
We can be used for forest fire detection, burst fire detection.

727
00:41:57,200 --> 00:42:00,300
You can do it for border patrol, pipeline patrol.

728
00:42:00,400 --> 00:42:05,320
We like the idea of calling this like a poor man's satellite.

729
00:42:05,420 --> 00:42:08,120
As new recruits get their wings at Edwards,

730
00:42:08,220 --> 00:42:09,960
old timers are retired.

731
00:42:09,961 --> 00:42:12,960
retired.

732
00:42:13,060 --> 00:42:17,360
In 2004, one of Edwards' most distinguished veterans,

733
00:42:17,460 --> 00:42:22,440
this B-52 that once carried the X-15 aloft, took off on its final

734
00:42:22,540 --> 00:42:27,620
flight from Edwards. Test pilot and former astronaut

735
00:42:27,720 --> 00:42:30,440
Gordon Fullerton was at the controls as the mother ship

736
00:42:30,540 --> 00:42:34,920
carried its latest fledgling, the X-43 scramjet, into the skies

737
00:42:35,020 --> 00:42:39,060
above Rogers' dry leg. After release from the bomber

